WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.360
Two drafts of the human genome appeared in 2001.

2
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:13.500
The first assembly with no gaps, for all but one chromosome, came in 2022.

3
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.060
The machines got faster every year, so why the wait?

4
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:20.660
Speed was never the bottleneck.

5
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.000
No instrument reads a chromosome in one pass.

6
00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:30.020
Every method reads pieces, and a genome is only as good as the way the pieces are

7
00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:31.020
put back together.

8
00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:33.513
First, count the pieces.

9
00:00:34.293 --> 00:00:37.673
A read is the sequence reported from one fragment.

10
00:00:38.593 --> 00:00:42.853
Coverage is how many reads, on average, lie over each base.

11
00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:48.413
Coverage equals reads times read length, divided by genome length.

12
00:00:49.313 --> 00:00:56.793
For a human genome of 3.1 billion bases, 30-fold coverage with 150-base reads

13
00:00:56.793 --> 00:00:59.853
needs about 620 million reads.

14
00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:06.203
Reads start at random positions, so 30-fold is a mean, not a guarantee.

15
00:01:07.283 --> 00:01:12.483
Under a Poisson model, the fraction of bases with no read is e to the minus C.

16
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:16.443
At one-fold, 37% is unread.

17
00:01:17.263 --> 00:01:20.663
At five-fold, about 21 million bases are unread.

18
00:01:21.383 --> 00:01:24.743
At 30-fold, far less than one base is expected.

19
00:01:25.263 --> 00:01:28.923
So more depth shrinks the gaps that random sampling leaves.

20
00:01:29.883 --> 00:01:32.743
Depth cannot fix a different problem.

21
00:01:33.503 --> 00:01:37.863
Give the genome two identical copies of a 6-kilobase repeat.

22
00:01:38.743 --> 00:01:42.983
A read from inside the repeat fits both copies equally well.

23
00:01:43.943 --> 00:01:45.643
Reads at its edges are unique.

24
00:01:46.363 --> 00:01:53.183
But no read, and no pair from a 400-base fragment, reaches across 6 kilobases to

25
00:01:53.183 --> 00:01:58.663
the far edge. So the assembler cannot tell which flanking sequences belong together.

26
00:01:59.483 --> 00:02:03.883
Deeper coverage adds more reads of the same ambiguous kind.

27
00:02:04.863 --> 00:02:07.763
Extra depth does not change what each read can say.

28
00:02:08.563 --> 00:02:12.563
Every new read from inside the repeat still fits both copies.

29
00:02:13.423 --> 00:02:18.123
The assembler collapses the two copies into one, or it breaks the contig.

30
00:02:18.867 --> 00:02:23.887
Now try a 15-kilobase read instead of a 150-base read.

31
00:02:24.587 --> 00:02:29.947
It can hold a whole 6-kilobase copy with several kilobases of unique sequence on

32
00:02:29.947 --> 00:02:32.747
each side. That pins the copy in place.

33
00:02:33.967 --> 00:02:37.087
Long reads span repeats that short reads cannot.

34
00:02:37.947 --> 00:02:43.307
Keep this rule: a repeat longer than your reads cannot be placed, and more depth

35
00:02:43.307 --> 00:02:44.027
does not help.
